]

phase('Read')
const results = await pipeline(
  TICKETS,
  (t) => agent(
    `You are producing a manual QA test procedure for a Zeo (KiCad fork) feature that is "In Review". Read the ACTUAL source to ground every detail — do not rely on assumptions.

Ticket: ${t.key} — ${t.name}

Read these files/areas:
${t.pointers.map( p => '  - ' + p ).join('\n')}

Produce a test procedure a QA engineer can follow. Critically:
- triggerSurface must state EXACTLY where the behavior shows up (e.g. "bottom message panel during interactive routing", "modal dialog", "window title") and which code path renders it. We already know some router features do NOT render in the drag overlay during fresh routing — be precise about whether the feature fires during interactive route (X key) vs drag (D key) vs DRC run.
- literalStrings: copy the exact wxString::Format templates or labels the code emits, verbatim, so QA can match them.
- caveats: call out anything partially implemented or that will NOT work. For B1b, list which of TRACK_WIDTH/VIA_DIAMETER/TRACK_ANGLE/DISALLOW/VIA_COUNT are actually evaluated. For C2d, state which of the 3 planned parts landed.
- prerequisites: what board/stackup/rule setup is needed first (e.g. impedance needs dielectrics with Dk; a track_width rule must be added in Board Setup → Custom Rules).
Return the structured object.`,
    { label: `read:${t.key}`, phase: 'Read', schema: PROC_SCHEMA }
  ),
  (proc) => proc
    ? agent(
        `Adversarially verify this manual test procedure against the ACTUAL Zeo source. Re-read the relevant files under ${ROOT} yourself; do not trust the procedure.

Procedure to check:
${JSON.stringify(proc, null, 2)}

Your job: would a QA engineer following these exact steps observe the stated expectedResult, GIVEN what the code actually does? Default to skeptical.
- Check the triggerSurface is real: does the feature actually render where the procedure says? (e.g. router status during fresh interactive routing vs only on drag/obstacle).
- Check literalStrings match the source verbatim.
- Check prerequisites are sufficient (e.g. will the calc return 0 / blank under some stackups?).
- Flag any step that will silently no-op.
Return the verdict with specific problems and corrected notes.`,
        { label: `verify:${proc.ticket}`, phase: 'Verify', schema: VERDICT_SCHEMA }
      ).then( v => ({ proc, verdict: v }) )
    : null
)

return results.filter(Boolean)

